      "problem": "What is $\\frac{5{h}^{2}-10h-75}{h+3}$ reduced to lowest terms?",
      "worked_solution": "The numerator can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{h}^{2}-10h-75=5({h}^{2}-2h-15)=5(h-5)(h+3).\n$$\n\nCanceling out any common factors that appear in the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{h}^{2}-10h-75}{h+3} & = \\\\ \\frac{5(h-5)(h+3)}{h+3} & = \\\\ \\frac{5(h-5)\\cancel{(h+3)}}{\\cancel{h+3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5(h-5)}{1} & = \\\\ 5h-25 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
